design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:96: - **Join:** the board parts of `design/bom.csv` to PCB1 in TWO solder passes, one per face, each with its own paste stencil and its own reflow. The board has parts on both faces (`design/ecad/ring/pcb/board-notes-r3.md:35`). PASS 1 is the SCREEN FACE and PASS 2 the PARTS FACE (`design/mfg/assembly/process.md:84`, `design/mfg/assembly/process.md:85`; confirmed by the lead, `design/wiring/lead-decisions.md:82`). PASS 1, the SCREEN FACE (KiCad B.Cu, 21 parts in `design/ecad/ring/pcb/fab/ring-pos-back.csv:1`): J1 (`screen_connector`, Hirose FH35C-15S-0.3SHW(50), `design/bom.csv:11`), the eight 020
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:97: - **The 15 ribbon lands:** J_BAND_A (8 lands) and J_BAND_B (7 lands) on the parts face get paste in PASS 2, one opening per land. Each land leaves the oven with a solder layer of about 0.03 mm. This layer is the solder of the hot-bar joint of step 14 (`design/mfg/assembly/hotbar-joint.md:23`; `design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:55`, `design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:137`). The board finish ENEPIG brings no solder of its own (`design/ecad/ring/pcb/fab/stackup.txt:20`). The delivered `ring-F_Paste.gbr` holds one 0.300 x 0.440 mm opening on each of the 15 lands (`design/ecad/ring/pcb/board-note
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:100: - **Never a third reflow:** J1 accepts at most 2 reflow cycles, and the two passes use both. Hirose's drawing says "Number of reflow cycles:2 cycles MAX." (`design/datasheets/w1_hirose_fh35c-15s-0.3shw50_drawing.pdf` page 6, an image page; `design/mfg/assembly/process.md:116`). So the ring board never goes through an oven a third time. The order of the passes: the face reflowed first hangs face down during the second reflow, and the melted solder holds a part there only if the part is small. The screen face has 9 parts, all small (eight 0201 parts and the connector); the parts face has 72, amo
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:103: - **Check:** every joint of each pass is judged by these accept rules, under the 10 times magnifier (the lead's wording, after the common workmanship practice for class 2 electronics, `design/wiring/lead-decisions.md:82`). A joint passes only when all of them hold. 1. The paste has melted and flowed: the solder is smooth and joined up, with no grainy paste left. 2. The solder wets both the land and the metal end or lead of the part: its edge runs out thin onto both, in a hollow curve. 3. No ball of solder sits on top of a land without wetting it, and no bare metal ring shows round the solder. 
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:116: - **Join:** nothing is joined, and nothing is cleaned. The board is not washed and not brushed, with or without a solvent. The microphone maker forbids it: "Do not board wash or clean after the reflow process." and "Do not brush board with or without solvents after the reflow process." (`design/mfg/assembly/process.md:71`). The paste residue is made to stay on the board (`design/mfg/assembly/process.md:70`). The solder domes on the 15 ribbon lands stay too: they are the solder of step 14 (`design/mfg/assembly/hotbar-joint.md:23`). This step is inspection only (change C8, `design/mfg/assembly/p
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:124: - **Join:** nothing is joined. A jig touches the SCREEN FACE, where the 12 test pads are, and the PARTS FACE, where the ribbon lands J_BAND_B are (`design/ecad/ring/pcb/placement-final.csv:80`; the jig holds the board between two sets of spring pins [PROPOSED: the lead and the jig maker confirm]). THE 12 TEST PADS, 0.80 mm round, on the screen face in a grid of 3 columns and 5 rows at a pitch of 1.05 mm, in the ring frame (X along the finger, `design/ecad/ring/pcb/placement-final.csv:87` to `design/ecad/ring/pcb/placement-final.csv:98`): column X -2.65: TP1 (Y -2.10), TP3 (Y -1.05), TP4 (Y 0.0
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:136: - **Check:** every switch joint passes the accept rules of step 2. Each switch closes its own line and no other, measured with an ohmmeter at the ribbon's board end (the FLEX1 foot) while it is pressed, and reads open (no connection) when it is released, so a switch that is closed all the time fails. The foot's lands carry the same numbers as J_BAND_A (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:65`); the flex drawing of each size, `design/ecad/band/fab/flex1-usN-drawing.pdf`, prints the number and net of each foot land (change C13, `design/mfg/assembly/process.md:207`). Probe between land 8 (GND) and
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:141: - **Join:** M1 (`vibration_motor`, Jinlong W0525AB001D) to FLEX1, the red lead to the + land and the blue lead to the - land, by hand soldering (`design/architecture.md:597`, `design/architecture.md:605`). FLEX1 has no mark on it. The + land is the land nearer the slot: land 1 of the motor lands, net VMOT, joined to land 7 (MOT+) of J_BAND_A. The - land is the other one: land 2, net GND, joined to land 6 (MOT-) of J_BAND_A (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:123`). The red lead is the + lead (`design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:24`).
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:150: - **Join:** CP1 (`pad_VBUS`), CP2 (`pad_D-`), CP3 (`pad_D+`), CP4 (`pad_GND`) to the four pad lands of FLEX2 (`band_flex_away`). The pad is a gold-plated disc, 1.6 mm across and 0.07 mm thick, soldered flat on its land with SAC305 paste at this step. The bare land is NOT the pad: its ENIG gold would wear through under the case's spring pins (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:141`; `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:30` to `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:33`). Four discs, one per land, in the order VBUS (CP1), D- (CP2), D+ (CP3), GND (CP4). Each disc has a via at the centre of its land (`des
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:154: - **Check:** each disc joint passes the accept rules of step 2. Each disc lies flat on its land and is centred on it, with no tilt (a lifted edge fails). Height: the disc top stands 0.070 to 0.112 mm above the land copper, measured with a feeler gauge or a microscope with a focus scale in steps of 0.02 mm (`design/mfg/assembly/process.md:163`, `design/mfg/assembly/process.md:181`). Continuity from each disc to its land at the ribbon's board end (the FLEX2 foot, lands numbered on the flex drawing `design/ecad/band/fab/flex2-usN-drawing.pdf`, change C13): VBUS to land 4, D- to land 5, D+ to land
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:159: - **Join:** the three output tabs of the bought cell pack (BT1 with PRT1 (`cell_protection`) and RT1 inside it as one part, `design/bom.csv:48`; `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:34` to `design/wiring/product-wiring.csv:36`) to the three lands of FLEX2, by hand soldering at low heat. RT1 is not a separate part and has no joint of its own. Tab P+ goes on land 1 (VBAT), tab NTC on land 3 (NTC), tab P- on land 2 (GND). The tabs lie in the order P+, NTC, P- from the slot toward the cell, which is lands 1, 3, 2 (`design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:111` to `design/ecad/band/band-interface.md:115`). 
design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md:163: - **Check:** the three joints pass the accept rules of step 2. The cell voltage appears at the ribbon's board end (the FLEX2 foot, lands numbered on the flex drawing as in step 8, change C13): red probe on land 1 (VBAT), black probe on land 2 (GND), with needle tips of 0.2 mm or finer (`design/mfg/assembly/process.md:180`). The reading must be positive. If it is negative, first check on the drawing that the probes sit on lands 1 and 2; only if they do is a tab on the wrong land. The thermistor inside the pack reads about 10 k between land 3 (NTC) and land 2 (GND) at room temperature (`design/b
